.\" XLandlord(6) - The Landlord's Game for X11
.\" Copyright (C) 2026 Lee Curtis
.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
.\"
.\" The manual page: what the program is, how to build, install and set it
.\" up (network play over ssh included), its preferences file, and the full
.\" rules of the game as XLandlord plays them. Made into XLandlord.6 by
.\" configure (the version number is filled in) and installed by `make install'.
.\"
.\" .TS / tbl is not used, so the page reads the same with any man(1).
.TH XLANDLORD 6 "2026" "XLandlord @VERSION@" "Games"
.SH NAME
XLandlord \- The Landlord's Game (Lizzie J. Magie, 1904) for X11, against the computer or over the network
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B XLandlord
.RB [ \-\-host " | " \-\-join
.RI [ user\fB@\fP] host [\fB:\fP port ]
.RB " | " \-\-local ]
.br
.B XLandlord
.BR \-\-version " | " \-\-help
.br
.B XLandlord \-\-relay
.I port
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B XLandlord
plays
.IR "The Landlord's Game" ,
the board game patented by Lizzie J. Magie in 1904 (US Patent 748,626)
and the ancestor of Monopoly, by the rules published by the Economic Game
Company of New York in 1906, including the
.B Single Tax
rules and the optional rules
.RI "for " "advanced and scientific players" .
.PP
Two to four players sit at the table. Each seat is a human at this screen
(players take turns at the keyboard), a computer player at an
.BR Easy ,
.B Medium
or
.B Hard
level, or a player on another machine who joins over
.BR ssh (1)
(see
.BR "NETWORK PLAY" ).
The window is drawn in the style of the Viola web browser: flat gray94
panels, raised bevels, black focus boxes, Helvetica menus.
.PP
This page covers installing and setting up the game, its preferences, and
the complete rules.
.B Help > How to Play
(F1) in the game is an illustrated guide to the same rules; the rule book
.I RULES.md
and
.I xlandlord\-rules.pdf
add screenshots, a numbered board map and the history of the game.
.SH OPTIONS
At start-up the game asks whether to
.BR "Host a Network Game" ,
.BR "Join a Network Game" ,
or
.BR "Play Here" .
These options skip the question:
.TP
.B \-\-host
Host a network game (see
.BR "NETWORK PLAY" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-join " \fR[\fPuser\fB@\fP\fR]\fPhost\fR[\fP\fB:\fPport\fR]\fP"
Join the game hosted on
.IR host :
an IPv4 or IPv6 address, or a host name found in DNS or
.IR /etc/hosts .
.IB user @
logs in to the host as another user;
.BI : port
is needed only when the host chose a port other than 1904. Write an IPv6
address with a port in brackets:
.BR [2001:db8::5]:1905 .
.TP
.B \-\-local
Play at this screen, against the computer or with others taking turns.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a short usage message and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-relay " port"
Run by ssh on the host for network play, never by hand: connect standard
input and output to the game hosted on this machine at
.BI 127.0.0.1: port\fR.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH INSTALLATION
XLandlord is written in C99 with plain Xlib. To build it you need a C
compiler,
.BR make (1),
and the X11 development headers and libraries
.RB ( libx11\-dev
on Debian and Ubuntu,
.B libX11\-devel
on Fedora,
.B libx11
on Arch; part of the X sets on NetBSD). The X server needs its standard
fonts
.RB ( xfonts\-base ,
.BR xfonts\-75dpi ).
.PP
From a release tarball:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tar xzf xlandlord\-@VERSION@.tar.gz
cd xlandlord\-@VERSION@
\&./configure
make
make check          # optional: the rules engine and network tests
sudo make install
.fi
.RE
.PP
From a git checkout, run
.B ./autogen.sh
first (it needs autoconf and automake). Useful
.B configure
options:
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix= dir
Install under
.I dir
instead of
.IR /usr/local .
.B \-\-prefix=$HOME/.local
installs for yourself only, without root.
.TP
.BI \-\-docdir= dir
Where the documents go (default
.IR prefix /share/doc/xlandlord ).
.PP
.B make install
puts the program in
.IR prefix /bin/XLandlord,
this manual page in
.IR prefix /share/man/man6/XLandlord.6,
a menu entry in
.IR prefix /share/applications/xlandlord.desktop,
icons under
.IR prefix /share/icons/hicolor
and
.IR prefix /share/pixmaps,
and
.IR README.md ,
.IR INSTALL.md ,
.IR RULES.md ,
.I xlandlord\-rules.pdf
and the rule book's pictures in the documentation directory.
.B make uninstall
removes them all again. The game can also be run without installing it:
.B ./XLandlord
in the build directory.
.PP
.I INSTALL.md
has the full guide: packages for each system, every make target, and
troubleshooting.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH SETTING UP NETWORK PLAY
Network play runs over ssh. Every machine needs the same version of
XLandlord installed, and each player who joins must be able to log in to
the host with
.B ssh
.I without typing a password
(the game runs ssh with
.BR BatchMode=yes ,
so ssh never stops to ask). The game does not set this up; the usual way is:
.IP 1. 4
On the host, run an ssh server
.RB ( "sudo apt install openssh\-server"
on Debian and Ubuntu;
.B sshd=YES
in
.I /etc/rc.conf
on NetBSD).
.IP 2. 4
On each joiner's machine, make a key pair if there is none:
.RS
.IP
.B ssh\-keygen \-t ed25519
.RE
.IP
Press Enter for an empty passphrase, or choose one and load the key into
.BR ssh\-agent (1)
with
.BR ssh\-add (1)
before playing.
.IP 3. 4
Install the public key on the host (this asks for the host password one
last time):
.RS
.IP
.B ssh\-copy\-id
.IB user @ host
.RE
.IP
Without
.BR ssh\-copy\-id ,
append
.I ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
to
.I ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
on the host.
.IP 4. 4
Log in once with a plain
.B ssh
.IB user @ host
to accept the host's key, then check that this prints the version without
asking anything:
.RS
.IP
.B ssh \-o BatchMode=yes
.IB user @ host
.B XLandlord \-\-version
.RE
.PP
If step 4 fails:
.TP
.B Permission denied
The key is not in the host's
.IR ~/.ssh/authorized_keys ,
or the permissions are too open: on the host,
.B chmod 700 ~/.ssh
and
.BR "chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys" .
.TP
.B Host key verification failed
Log in once with a plain
.BR ssh .
.TP
.B command not found
Non-interactive ssh does not see the host's PATH (for instance
.IR /usr/local/bin ).
Fix the PATH for non-interactive shells on the host, or set
.B net_command
to the program's full path on the host in the joiner's preferences (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.PP
To join by host name, the joiner's machine must find the name in DNS or in
.IR /etc/hosts ,
for example
.PP
.RS
.B 192.168.1.20   gamebox
.RE
.PP
or the joiner types the host's IP address instead. A different ssh user,
port or key for the host goes in a
.B Host
entry in the joiner's
.IR ~/.ssh/config ;
the game uses whatever ssh does.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH NETWORK PLAY
One player chooses
.B Host a Network Game
(at start-up or in the Game menu), picks the computer player's strength,
the length of the game, the rules and a port (1904 unless that is taken),
and waits in the waiting room. The waiting room shows how to reach the
host: its host name and its IP addresses.
.PP
Up to three others choose
.B Join a Network Game
and type the host's address,
.RI [ user\fB@\fP] host [\fB:\fP port ].
A name is looked up first, in DNS and
.IR /etc/hosts ;
one found in neither is reported
.RB ( "Host Not Found" )
before ssh is started. The joiner's game then runs
.PP
.RS
.B ssh \-T \-o BatchMode=yes
.IB user @ host
.B XLandlord \-\-relay
.I port
.RE
.PP
and the relay connects to the hosting game, which listens on 127.0.0.1
only. The host runs the only real game; joiners see the same board and the
same dialogs, and on their turn the buttons work. When everyone is in, the
host presses
.BR "Start Game" .
.PP
Next to each player's name the game shows his machine: the host's own host
name, or for a joiner the name of the machine his ssh connection came from
(the relay reads the address from ssh's
.B SSH_CONNECTION
and the host looks it up in its
.I /etc/hosts
and DNS), or its IP address when it has no name.
.PP
Four players at most: a fifth is turned away with a warning, and so is
anyone who tries to join a game already under way. When fewer than four
humans play, one computer player takes a free seat; with four humans there
is none. If a joiner's connection drops, a medium computer player takes
over his seat; if the host leaves, the game ends for everyone.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH CONFIGURATION
The game's settings are kept in
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xlandlord/preferences.conf
(normally
.IR ~/.config/xlandlord/preferences.conf ),
written whenever a setting is changed in the game. It holds
.I key " = " value
lines; lines starting with
.B #
are comments, and unknown keys are ignored. The file may be edited by hand
while the game is not running.
.TP
.BR seat1 " .. " seat4
What sits in each seat of the New Game form:
.BR empty ,
.BR human ,
.BR easy ,
.B medium
or
.BR hard .
A table needs at least two players and one human.
.TP
.BR name1 " .. " name4
The names of the human seats.
.TP
.B end_wages
How many times a player draws his wages before the game ends:
.BR 3 ,
.B 5
(the 1906 rule, the default),
.B 7
or
.BR 10 .
.TP
.B advanced
.B 1
for the advanced and scientific rules,
.B 0
for the standard rules.
.TP
.B ask_bids
.B 1
to be asked for a bid when another player stops on an unowned card
(Options > Ask Me to Bid),
.B 0
not to bid.
.TP
.B confirm_cpu
.B 1
to wait for a key after every computer move (Options > Confirm Each
Computer Move).
.TP
.B speed
The pause after each computer move:
.B 0
fast,
.B 1
normal,
.B 2
slow (Options > Computer Speed).
.TP
.B net_host
The last host joined, offered again by Join a Network Game.
.TP
.B net_port
The port to host on (default 1904).
.TP
.B net_command
The program ssh runs on the host (default
.BR XLandlord ).
Give its full path, such as
.BR /usr/local/bin/XLandlord ,
when it is not on the PATH that non-interactive ssh sees on the host.
.TP
.B net_cpu
The strength of the computer player in a hosted game:
.BR easy ,
.B medium
or
.BR hard .
.PP
A preferences file left by version 0.0.6 or earlier, when the game was
called Viola the Landlord
.RI ( ~/.config/viola\-landlord/preferences.conf ),
is read once and saved under the new name.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH THE RULES
These are the rules of 1906 as XLandlord plays them. Rule numbers are
those of the 1906 rule sheet.
.SS The object
Get as much wealth as you can. The game ends when one player has drawn his
wages five times (or the number chosen), and the richest player wins.
Wealth is counted as your cash, plus
.B 100
for every card you hold (title deeds, railroad and franchise charters, the
broker's license) whatever you paid for it, plus 100 for every house you
built, every luxury card, and every Professor card (Single Tax only).
A lot bought for $25 is thus worth $75 more than it cost before it collects
a single rent.
.SS Starting
Every player starts on MOTHER EARTH with $600 if two play, $500 if three,
$400 if four.
.SS The deal (Rule 1)
The cards are shuffled and dealt round, one at a time, until 24 have been
dealt or the pack is empty (there are 23 cards). Each player may buy any of
the cards dealt to him at its price; the money goes into the PUBLIC
TREASURY. Cards not bought go back into the pack. The rules advise keeping
$50 to $75 for expenses around the board.
.SS A turn
Before throwing you may build houses, buy or sell cards, or propose the
Single Tax. Then throw two dice and move forward that many spaces, except:
.TP
.B Between the Chances (Rule 2)
On one of the six spaces between the Chance at 32 and the Chance at 39 you
may move backward instead.
.TP
.B The railroad pass (Rule 17)
A double is an official pass on the railroad: if the throw carries you past
the next corner you may jump the nine spaces to the following corner,
finishing nine spaces further on. You may decline it.
.TP
.B Refusing to speculate (Rule 8)
If the throw would bring you onto SPECULATION you may refuse to move.
.PP
.B Wages (Rule 3):
each time you reach or pass MOTHER EARTH you draw $100.
Several players may stand on one space; each pays what it calls for.
.SS The board
Forty spaces: four corners and nine on each side, played from MOTHER EARTH
(bottom right) right to left along the bottom, up the left side, across the
top and down the right side. A railroad stands in the middle of each side.
.PP
The lots, with price and land rent:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ta 4n 26n 34n
 1	Lonely Lane	$25	$2
 3	Goat Alley	$50	$4
 6	The Pike	$25	$2
 8	Market Place	$50	$4
 9	Cottage Terrace	$50	$6
12	Boomtown	$50	$6
14	Beggarman's Court	$50	$6
17	Easy Street	$75	$8
19	The Bowery	$75	$10
23	Fairhope Avenue	$75	$10
26	George Street	$75	$12
28	Madison Square	$100	$14
31	Grand Boulevard	$100	$16
33	Wall Street	$100	$18
36	Broadway	$100	$20
38	Fifth Avenue	$100	$22
.fi
.RE
.PP
The other cards cost $50 each: the railroads (P.D.Q. R.R. at 5, Gee Whiz
R.R. at 15, Shooting Star R.R. at 25, Royal Rusher R.R. at 35), the
franchises (Soakum Lighting System at 16, Slambang Trolley at 22) and the
broker's license (Speculation, at 21).
.PP
The other spaces: the four
.B Absolute Necessities
(Food, Shelter, Fuel, Clothing) charge a $10 tax; at
.B Lord Blueblood's Estate
and
.B D. F. Hogg's Game Preserves
you are trespassing and go to Jail;
.B Chance
draws a card;
.B Luxury
sells a luxury;
.B Legacy
pays you $100;
.B Central Park
is free; stopping on
.B Jail
or the
.B Poor House
is only a visit.
.SS Buying, bidding and rent (Rule 7)
When you stop on a lot, railroad or franchise owned by another player, you
pay him its land rent (plus $10 for each house on it), or its fare or
charge. If nobody owns it, it is for sale at its price and the other
players may bid more, in $5 steps. You have the first option, at the
highest bid (or at the price if nobody bid). If you do not buy and somebody
bid, he buys it and you pay him the rent at once; if nobody bid, nobody
buys it, and you pay its land rent (or $5 for a railroad or franchise) into
the Public Treasury.
.SS Railroads and franchises (Rule 9)
Each stop by another player pays the owner $5 for one railroad; $10 on each
when he owns two (a MONOPOLY); $20 on each for three; $50 on each for all
four. A franchise pays $5; both franchises (the MUNICIPAL CINCH) pay $25
each. If two players own all four railroads between them they may pool them
in a
.BR TRUST :
every fare becomes $40, the partners divide the profits and ride free on
each other's lines. A trust breaks up when a railroad changes hands.
.SS Taxes, Chance, Luxury, Legacy (Rules 4, 11, 13)
The Absolute Necessities charge $10 each, paid into the Public Treasury.
.PP
A
.B Chance
card is drawn from a pack of sixteen and put back afterwards. They pay you
$25, $30, $50 or $100, $5 a lot you own, or $10 from every other player;
cost you $10, $25 or $50, $10 a house, or $10 to every other player; or move
you to Jail, to Mother Earth (with wages), to Central Park, to the next
railroad (paying the fare), or back three spaces. Money paid goes into the
Public Treasury.
.PP
At a
.B Luxury
pay $75 for a luxury card, which counts 100 at the end. If you will not or
cannot, move backward from where the throw began, as far as you threw, and
settle that space instead.
.SS Speculation (Rule 8)
If nobody holds the broker's license it is first offered for sale. You pay
a $10 ante to the broker (to the bank if there is none) and throw again:
any double wins $100; 11 wins $90, 10 $80, 9 $70, 8 $60, 7 $50, 6 $40,
5 $30; 3 or 4 is a
.I skin game
and wins nothing. The broker takes 10% of your winnings; on a skin game he
is caught, goes to Jail, and his license goes back to the pack.
.SS Jail (Rules 5 and 6)
Trespassing, or the Chance card that says so, sends you to Jail and ends
your turn. On each later turn either pay the $50 fine and throw as usual,
or throw for a double: a double lets you out and you move by it. On the
third turn the fine is paid for you. You leave Jail counting from Shelter.
.SS Raising money and the Poor House (Rule 12)
When you owe more than you have you must sell: a card to a computer player
at his offer, a card back to the pack for half its price, or a house to the
bank for $50 (houses go before the lot under them). If you cannot pay and
have nothing left to sell, you pay what you have and go to the Poor House
until your next turn.
.SS Houses (Rule 14)
Before throwing you may build on your own lots. A house costs $100, adds
$10 to the lot's rent, and counts 100 at the end. Up to nine houses stand
on a lot (three under the advanced rules).
.SS Trading (Rule 16)
All cards may be bought, sold and traded between players before a throw. A
computer player accepts an offer that beats what the card is worth to it;
a human is asked to accept or decline. Lots with houses cannot change hands
until the houses are sold.
.SS The end (Rule 19)
The game ends at the end of the turn in which a player draws his wages for
the last time. The final standings list each player's cash, cards, houses,
luxuries and worth; the richest player is the winner.
.SS The advanced and scientific rules
With 10 houses on the board the tax on each Absolute Necessity is $20, with
25 houses $40. The corners and railroads divide the board into eight
sections of four spaces; when every lot in a section has a house its land
rents double, at two houses each they double again, at three again. At most
three houses stand on a lot.
.SS The Single Tax
At any time in his turn a player may propose the Single Tax; it comes into
force with the votes of at least two players (his own and one more). Then
title deeds to lots are worth nothing, and:
.IP \(bu 3
there are no taxes on the Absolute Necessities;
.IP \(bu 3
all land rent goes into the Public Treasury, which starts empty; an owner
of houses on the lot still collects $10 a house, and an unimproved lot may
be built on by whoever stops there;
.IP \(bu 3
railroad fares and franchise charges are paid to their owners until the
public buys them;
.IP \(bu 3
every $50 in the Treasury is spent for the public, in order: Soakum
Lighting, Slambang Trolley, the four railroads (each bought from its owner
for $50 and then free), a free college on Lord Blueblood's Estate and one
on Hogg's Game Preserves (no more Jail there), and after that $10 more
wages for every $50;
.IP \(bu 3
stopping at a free college gives an Education card; four make a Professor
card, which counts 100 at the end;
.IP \(bu 3
there is no Poor House: a player who cannot pay rests on the corner behind
him and draws $50 wages from it before his next throw.
.SS The computer players
.B Easy
plays on impulse: buys about half of what it can afford, seldom bids or
builds.
.B Medium
plays by rules of thumb, keeping $100 in hand.
.B Hard
counts: it values each card by the rent it should bring, keeps in hand what
a trip round the board is likely to cost, chases railroad monopolies and
blocks yours, and votes on the Single Tax by what it does to its standing.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH PLAYING AT THE SCREEN
On your turn the
.B Roll Dice
button has the black focus box; press it, Space or Enter. Whenever the
rules give you a choice \(em buying a lot, bidding, paying the jail fine,
using the pass, moving backward, speculating, buying a luxury \(em a dialog
asks. Build, Trade and the Single Tax are in the
.B Game
menu and on the buttons. Click a space on the board to read about it.
When several humans share the screen, a
.B Pass the game to ...
prompt appears between their turns, and every question names the player
it is for.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BR Space ", " Enter ", " R
roll the dice
.TP
.B B
build a house
.TP
.BR T " / " S
buy a card from, or sell a card to, another player
.TP
.B X
propose the Single Tax
.TP
.B F1
how to play (the illustrated guide)
.TP
.B F2
the standings
.TP
.BR PgUp " / " PgDn " / " End
scroll the record of events
.TP
.BR Esc ", " F9 ", " F10
the menu bar; Alt+G, Alt+O, Alt+H open a menu
.TP
.BR Ctrl+N " / " Ctrl+Q
new game / quit
.PP
In dialogs Enter presses the button with the focus box, Tab and the arrow
keys move it, Esc backs out, and an underlined letter presses its button.
In lists Space ticks a box. While the computer players move, any key
hurries them along.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/xlandlord/preferences.conf
The preferences (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/doc/xlandlord/
The rule book
.RI ( RULES.md ,
.IR xlandlord\-rules.pdf ),
.I README.md
and
.IR INSTALL.md .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
The X display to use.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the preferences folder lives (default
.IR ~/.config ).
.TP
.B SSH_CONNECTION
Set by sshd for the relay; tells the host which machine a joiner comes
from.
.TP
.B XLANDLORD_SSH
For testing: a program to run instead of
.BR ssh .
.SH NOTES
The board follows the layout of Magie's 1904 patent drawing, and the rules
are those of the patent and of 1906; both are in the public domain. The
exact order of some spaces, the land rents and the Chance cards are not
given by the surviving rule text and are reconstructed; see
.I README.md
and section 20 of the rule book. Every picture in the program is original
pixel art.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh\-keygen (1),
.BR ssh\-copy\-id (1),
.BR hosts (5),
.BR X (7)
